# Service lifecycle logs (anti-flake clause 5)

The AIC PIM workspace's daemons are managed by the platform's
service supervisor (Windows SCM, systemd, launchd). When a
customer support engineer or government auditor opens the
Windows Event Viewer (or `journalctl -u pim-offline-agent`,
or `log show --predicate 'process == "pim-offline-agent"'` on
macOS), they expect to see a **three-record story** for every
service lifetime: started, stopping, stopped. The records must
carry enough context to answer:

- "When did this service start, with what version, talking to
 which endpoint?" → `service started`.
- "What was the daemon doing when it began shutdown? Was a
 sync in flight?" → `service stopping`.
- "Did the drain finish cleanly within budget?" → `service stopped`.

The post-2026-05-19 win32 configurator audit caught services
that emitted only the generic
`PROCESS_STARTED` / `PROCESS_STOPPED` records from
`pim-windows-oplog::ids` (IDs 41001 / 41002) with no service-
lifecycle context. An auditor reading those records could not
tell which `pim-offline-server` version was running, which
endpoint it was advertising, or whether shutdown was clean.
This rule pins the three-record contract.

## The rule

> **Every binary registered as a Windows service, a systemd
> unit, or a launchd job emits, at `tracing::Level::INFO`,
> three records — `service.started`, `service.stopping`,
> `service.stopped` — to BOTH the Windows Event Log (via
> `pim-windows-oplog`) AND the rolling `tracing` log. The
> Windows Event Log entries use IDs from the lifecycle range
> `41001-41099` (concrete allocation below). The payload
> shape for each record is fixed by this rule.**

The existing `PROCESS_STARTED` (41001) and `PROCESS_STOPPED`
(41002) IDs continue to mark the generic process boundary
(useful for non-service binaries — configurator CLIs,
diagnostics tools). Services additionally emit the richer
triplet below.

## Event ID allocation (lifecycle range 41001-41099)

| ID | Constant (in `pim-windows-oplog::ids`) | Emitted by |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 41001 | `PROCESS_STARTED` | Every binary (existing) |
| 41002 | `PROCESS_STOPPED` | Every binary (existing) |
| 41003 | `STARTUP_FAILED` | Every binary (existing) |
| 41004 | `OPERATIONAL_WARNING` | Every binary (existing) |
| 41005 | `OPERATIONAL_ERROR` | Every binary (existing) |
| **41010** | **`SERVICE_STARTED`** | Services only (new — this rule) |
| **41011** | **`SERVICE_STOPPING`** | Services only (new — this rule) |
| **41012** | **`SERVICE_STOPPED`** | Services only (new — this rule) |

41006-41009 are reserved for future generic-process records
(e.g. a "configuration reload" event); 41013-41099 are reserved
for future service-lifecycle records (e.g. health-degraded /
health-restored beacons). Adding a new ID in the range requires
bumping the message catalog (`pim-windows-oplog-messages/oplog_messages.mc`)
and registering the ID in `declared_event_ids()` per the
existing `pim-windows-oplog` contract.

## Required payload shape — `service.started` (41010)

Emitted as the first action after `pim_shutdown` handler
installation completes and BEFORE any long-running task spawns.
Both the Windows Event Log entry and the `tracing::info!`
record carry these fields:

| Field | Type | Source |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `service` | string | the binary's program id (`pim-offline-server`, `pim-offline-agent`, …) |
| `pid` | u32 | `std::process::id()` |
| `exe` | string | `std::env::current_exe()` (path) |
| `version` | string | `env!("CARGO_PKG_VERSION")` |
| `git_sha` | string | `option_env!("PIM_BUILD_GIT_SHA").unwrap_or("unknown")` (set by `build.rs`) |
| `config_store_kind` | string | `"registry-hklm"` / `"libsecret+toml"` / `"keychain+plist"` |
| `service_uri` | string | the daemon's primary listening URL (server) or its peer URL (agent) — empty if not applicable |
| `machine_guid` | string | the manifest's `MACHINE_GUID` value if set, else empty (R6) |
| `fips_posture` | string | `pim_crypto_rustls_bridge::fips_status()` rendered as `"validated:4759"` / `"pending"` / `"native-tls:schannel"` etc. |
| `warnings_count` | u32 | `runtime_status.warnings.len()` at startup (R7) |
| `tenant_count` | u32 | server only — number of tenants the SQL store knows about; agents omit this field |

Example `tracing::info!`:

```rust
tracing::info!(
    op = "service.started",
    service = %SERVICE_ID,
    pid = std::process::id(),
    exe = %std::env::current_exe()?.display(),
    version = env!("CARGO_PKG_VERSION"),
    git_sha = option_env!("PIM_BUILD_GIT_SHA").unwrap_or("unknown"),
    config_store_kind = %config_store_kind_str(),
    service_uri = %service_uri_redacted_or_empty(),
    machine_guid = %resolved.machine_guid_or_empty(),
    fips_posture = %fips_status_str(),
    warnings_count = warnings.len() as u32,
    "service started",
);
```

The Windows Event Log mirror uses `pim_windows_oplog::emit(ids::SERVICE_STARTED, …)`
with the same field set (rendered into the message catalog
template for ID 41010).

## Required payload shape — `service.stopping` (41011)

Emitted from the shutdown handler the moment the shutdown
channel fires, BEFORE the drain step begins. This is the record
the auditor needs to answer "what was in flight when stop
arrived?".

| Field | Type | Source |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `service` | string | same as above |
| `reason` | string | `"signal:SIGTERM"` / `"signal:SIGINT"` / `"scm:stop"` / `"scm:preshutdown"` / `"scm:shutdown"` / `"close-requested"` / `"internal:fatal"` |
| `pid` | u32 | `std::process::id()` |
| `uptime_s` | u64 | seconds since `service.started` was emitted |
| `last_sync_age_s` | u64 | seconds since last successful sync (`u64::MAX` if never) — agent only; server omits |
| `consecutive_failures` | u32 | the scheduler's failure counter (R8 worked example) — agent only |
| `in_flight_requests` | array | one entry per outbound HTTP request still pending, `{ url: String, elapsed_ms: u64 }` — URL redacted per `pim_app_config::windows_oplog::redact::redact_url` (passwords stripped) |
| `inflight_count` | u32 | `in_flight_requests.len()` (for SIEM filters that don't want to parse arrays) |

Example:

```rust
tracing::info!(
    op = "service.stopping",
    service = %SERVICE_ID,
    reason = %reason,
    pid = std::process::id(),
    uptime_s = started_at.elapsed().as_secs(),
    last_sync_age_s = state.last_sync_age().as_secs(),
    consecutive_failures = state.consecutive_failures,
    inflight_count = inflight.len() as u32,
    in_flight_requests = ?inflight,
    "service stopping",
);
```

## Required payload shape — `service.stopped` (41012)

Emitted as the very last `tracing::info!` (and very last
`pim_windows_oplog::emit`) call before `std::process::exit` (or
before `main` returns). Mirrors the per-component drain budgets
documented in [`shutdown-compliance.mdc`](shutdown-compliance.mdc).

| Field | Type | Source |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `service` | string | same as above |
| `exit_code` | i32 | the value passed to `std::process::exit` (`0` for clean drain) |
| `drain_total_ms` | u64 | wall-clock from `service.stopping` to this record |
| `drain_steps` | array | one entry per `pim_shutdown::drain::bounded` step: `{ name: String, elapsed_ms: u64, outcome: "ok" | "timeout" | "error" }` |
| `aborted_steps` | u32 | count of `drain_steps[].outcome != "ok"` |
| `platform_budget_ms` | u64 | the platform's drain budget for this signal class (5_000 for SCM_STOP, 180_000 for PRESHUTDOWN, etc.) |

Example:

```rust
tracing::info!(
    op = "service.stopped",
    service = %SERVICE_ID,
    exit_code = exit_code,
    drain_total_ms = drain_started.elapsed().as_millis() as u64,
    aborted_steps = drain_results.iter().filter(|s| s.outcome != "ok").count() as u32,
    platform_budget_ms = platform_budget.as_millis() as u64,
    drain_steps = ?drain_results,
    "service stopped",
);
```

## Forbidden constructs

- A service whose only lifecycle record is `PROCESS_STARTED` /
 `PROCESS_STOPPED`. Services additionally emit the three
 41010 / 41011 / 41012 records.
- `tracing::info!("service started")` without the structured
 field set above. The message string is the operator-facing
 summary; the fields are the SIEM-facing payload. Both must
 be present.
- A `service.stopping` record that omits the `in_flight_requests`
 array. The whole reason the record exists is to make
 "what was the daemon doing when stop arrived?" answerable
 without a debugger.
- A `service.stopped` record emitted with `exit_code = 0` when
 any drain step's `outcome` is `"timeout"` or `"error"`. A
 timed-out drain is still a non-clean shutdown — surface it
 in the exit code (use `pim_shutdown::drain::ExitCode::DRAIN_TIMEOUT`,
 conventionally `75` per `sysexits.h` EX_TEMPFAIL or a
 product-pinned value).
- A Windows Event Log emit that bypasses `pim-windows-oplog`
 (e.g. a direct `ReportEventW` call). Always go through the
 shared crate so the message catalog DLL is kept honest by
 the self-test.
- A `tracing::info!("service started")` BEFORE the
 `pim_shutdown` handler is installed. The handler must be
 the first thing in `main()`; the lifecycle log comes after
 installation completes so a fast-arriving signal is already
 observable.

## Worked violation → fix

**Violation** (today, conceptual):

```rust
#[tokio::main]
async fn main() -> Result<()> {
    tracing_subscriber::init();
    tracing::info!("starting pim-offline-agent");

    let cfg = load_config()?;
    let http = HttpClient::new(&cfg)?;
    let scheduler = Scheduler::spawn(http, cfg);

    tokio::signal::ctrl_c().await?;
    scheduler.shutdown().await;
    Ok(())
}
```

No `service.started` with version, no `service.stopping` with
reason, no `service.stopped` with drain elapsed. An auditor
opening Event Viewer sees only a generic
`Service Control Manager` start/stop pair — useless for
forensic correlation across the fleet.

**Fix**:

```rust
#[tokio::main]
async fn main() -> Result<()> {
    tracing_subscriber::init();

    let (shutdown_tx, mut shutdown_rx) = tokio::sync::watch::channel(false);
    pim_shutdown::install_unix_signal_handler(shutdown_tx.clone())?;
    #[cfg(windows)]
    pim_shutdown::install_console_ctrl_handler(shutdown_tx.clone())?;

    let cfg = load_config()?;
    let warnings = runtime_status::fingerprint_warnings(&cfg);
    let http = HttpClient::new(&cfg)?;
    let started_at = std::time::Instant::now();

    pim_windows_oplog::emit(ids::SERVICE_STARTED, &service_started_payload(&cfg, &warnings))?;
    tracing::info!(
        op = "service.started",
        service = "pim-offline-agent",
        pid = std::process::id(),
        exe = %std::env::current_exe()?.display(),
        version = env!("CARGO_PKG_VERSION"),
        git_sha = option_env!("PIM_BUILD_GIT_SHA").unwrap_or("unknown"),
        config_store_kind = %cfg.store_kind(),
        service_uri = %cfg.server_uri_redacted(),
        machine_guid = %cfg.machine_guid_or_empty(),
        fips_posture = %pim_crypto_rustls_bridge::fips_status_str(),
        warnings_count = warnings.len() as u32,
        "service started",
    );

    let scheduler = Scheduler::spawn(http.clone(), cfg.clone(), shutdown_rx.clone());
    let _ = shutdown_rx.changed().await;

    let reason = pim_shutdown::reason_of_last_signal();
    let inflight = http.snapshot_inflight();
    pim_windows_oplog::emit(ids::SERVICE_STOPPING, &service_stopping_payload(&inflight, reason))?;
    tracing::info!(
        op = "service.stopping",
        service = "pim-offline-agent",
        reason = %reason,
        pid = std::process::id(),
        uptime_s = started_at.elapsed().as_secs(),
        last_sync_age_s = scheduler.last_sync_age_s(),
        consecutive_failures = scheduler.consecutive_failures(),
        inflight_count = inflight.len() as u32,
        in_flight_requests = ?inflight,
        "service stopping",
    );

    let drain_started = std::time::Instant::now();
    let drain_results = scheduler.drain(PLATFORM_BUDGET).await;
    let exit_code = if drain_results.iter().any(|s| s.outcome != "ok") { 75 } else { 0 };

    pim_windows_oplog::emit(ids::SERVICE_STOPPED, &service_stopped_payload(exit_code, &drain_results, PLATFORM_BUDGET))?;
    tracing::info!(
        op = "service.stopped",
        service = "pim-offline-agent",
        exit_code,
        drain_total_ms = drain_started.elapsed().as_millis() as u64,
        aborted_steps = drain_results.iter().filter(|s| s.outcome != "ok").count() as u32,
        platform_budget_ms = PLATFORM_BUDGET.as_millis() as u64,
        drain_steps = ?drain_results,
        "service stopped",
    );

    std::process::exit(exit_code);
}
```

The auditor's Event Viewer now shows IDs 41010 / 41011 / 41012
with the full payload; the rolling log carries the same fields
under structured keys for Loki / Splunk / Datadog correlation.

## Where this is currently enforced

| Binary | Status |
| --- | --- |
| `pim-offline-server` (Windows SCM service + systemd) | partial — emits PROCESS_STARTED / PROCESS_STOPPED; service-lifecycle triplet pending |
| `pim-offline-agent` (Windows SCM service + systemd + launchd) | partial — same |
| `pim-offline-{client,server}-configurator-{win32,tauri}` | n/a — configurators are not services; they emit per-action audit events instead |
| `pim-offline-client-elevate-{win32,tauri}` | n/a — modal helpers, not services |
| `pim-product-launcher-{slint,win32}` | n/a — not services |
| `pim-windows-oplog::ids::SERVICE_STARTED/STOPPING/STOPPED` constants (41010/41011/41012) | new — Wave 1 ID allocation; message catalog entry pending |

